\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A simple largest eigenvalue of a symmetric nonnegative matrix}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{A} be a symmetric matrix of fixed finite size, with nonnegative
entries in an ordered field. Write #{\langle x,y\rangle} for the standard
dot product on its index space. Assume that #{A} has a largest eigenvalue
#{\lambda} in the ordered field, that #{\langle x,Ax\rangle\le\lambda\langle x,x\rangle}
for every nonzero vector #{x}, and that equality holds exactly when
#{Ax=\lambda x}. Let the support graph have the indices as vertices and an
undirected edge between distinct indices exactly where the corresponding
entry is positive, and assume that this graph is connected.}

\p{Then #{\lambda} is simple, #{\lambda} is at least the absolute value of
every eigenvalue in the ordered field, and #{\lambda} has an entrywise
positive eigenvector. If some power of #{A} is entrywise positive, every
other eigenvalue has strictly smaller absolute value.}

\proof{
\p{If the index set has one element, the matrix is a nonnegative scalar,
its eigenvalue is simple, and a positive eigenvector is immediate. Now
suppose there are at least two indices. Connectedness supplies an edge,
so some off-diagonal entry #{A_{ij}} is positive. The vector with those
two coordinates equal to #{1}, and the others equal to #{0}, has positive
quadratic form. Thus #{\lambda>0}.}

\p{Let #{v} be a nonzero eigenvector for #{\lambda}, and let #{|v|} be its
entrywise absolute value. Nonnegativity gives
#{\lvert\langle v,Av\rangle\rvert\le\langle|v|,A|v|\rangle}, because each
summand is bounded by the corresponding summand of the second form. The
Rayleigh hypothesis bounds the second form by #{\lambda\langle v,v\rangle}.
Since #{\langle v,Av\rangle=\lambda\langle v,v\rangle} and #{\lambda>0},
both inequalities are equalities. Hence #{A|v|=\lambda|v|}. Equality of
the two forms forces #{v_av_b\ge0} whenever #{A_{ab}>0}.}

\p{The eigenvector equation for #{|v|} forces every neighbor of a zero
coordinate to vanish as well. The set of nonzero coordinates is therefore
a union of connected components. It is nonempty, so #{|v|} is entrywise
positive. The sign condition and connectedness then give #{v} a single
strict sign. Replacing #{v} by its negative if needed produces an
entrywise positive eigenvector #{p}.}

\p{For an eigenvalue #{\mu} with nonzero eigenvector #{w},
#{\lvert\mu\rvert\langle w,w\rangle
=\lvert\langle w,Aw\rangle\rvert
\le\langle|w|,A|w|\rangle
\le\lambda\langle w,w\rangle},
so #{\lvert\mu\rvert\le\lambda}. If #{u} is any eigenvector for
#{\lambda}, the scalar #{\langle u,p\rangle/\langle p,p\rangle} is defined
because #{p} is entrywise positive. Subtracting that multiple of #{p}
gives an eigenvector orthogonal to #{p}. The absolute-value argument
makes its absolute value an eigenvector, hence entrywise positive or
zero, and the sign argument gives it one strict sign unless it is zero.
A vector of one strict sign is not orthogonal to #{p}. Thus the
difference vanishes and the eigenspace is the line through #{p}.}

\p{Suppose a power #{A^s} is entrywise positive, and let #{\mu} be an
eigenvalue with #{\lvert\mu\rvert=\lambda} and eigenvector #{w\ne0}.
The comparison already proved gives equality, so #{|w|} is an entrywise
positive eigenvector for #{\lambda}. Then #{A^s|w|=\lambda^s|w|}, while
#{A^sw=\mu^sw}, and #{\lvert\mu\rvert^s=\lambda^s}. Nonnegativity of the
entries of #{A^s} gives
#{\lvert\langle w,A^sw\rangle\rvert\le\langle|w|,A^s|w|\rangle}.
Both sides equal #{\lambda^s\langle w,w\rangle}, so every summand
#{(A^s)_{ab}w_aw_b} has one common sign. Every entry of #{A^s} is
positive and #{|w|} has no zero, so #{w} itself has one strict sign:
#{w=\pm|w|}. Combined with #{A|w|=\lambda|w|} and #{Aw=\mu w}, each
choice of sign forces #{\mu=\lambda}.}
}

\p{The Rayleigh hypothesis is the part of the real spectral theorem used
here. \ref{fdip-011Y} records its source. The bound and the positive
eigenvector are the symmetric nonnegative case of the theorem associated
with Perron and with Frobenius; the argument above uses the ordering,
finite sums and connectedness.}
